      "is_best": false,
      "html": "<div class=\"post__content post__content--new-editor\"><p>Hi ​<user-mention data-id=\"50084\">@KHarb.1</user-mention> </p><p>I saw your post 2 years ago here <a href=\"https://community.st.com/stm32-mcus-products-25/can-overlapping-in-out-pins-be-used-to-internally-route-peripherals-on-stm32g4-133972\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_self\">Can overlapping IN\\OUT pins be used to internally route peripherals on STM32G4? | Community</a></p><p>I’m still not sure if you missed to provide more details in this pos. </p><p>To answer your question directly: No, STM32G4 COMP external outputs cannot internally drive UART RX on the same pin just because the two functions overlap there; you must use another routing method, typically an external wire. So, IN/OUT pin definitions do not create an internal hardware route by themselves </p></div>"
